## Summary

Performance audit of heliosian at f686822. Who's directory and the shared nav rail were walking the whole GROUP table on first load.

Shipped: the directory probes the kinds it lists; room-parent groups are a second probed query; the rail's running lists probe `(descendants …)` of groups whose managed_by holds the viewer, with `manages` kept as a recheck. `(descendants S)` is the rows in S and every row under them. A scalar seed's memo key includes the outer row ids it reads. Each visited row and each child-index lookup counts toward the existing 10-million-row budget, so a correlated tree walk past that budget is refused with the same error as any other over-wide query.

Measured on a laptop serving production sheets, one process, five interleaved rounds. Parent Who batch median 80.0ms to 57.2ms; owner 48.3ms to 30.5ms. Rows and order unchanged. The same batch without descendants, measured on production, went from 246.2ms to 240.0ms and was rejected. On the synthetic school the old rail walk read-checks GROUP 2966 times and the shipped navManaged query 6. BenchmarkSidebar runs the shipped navManaged, navJoined, groups, roomGroups, and effective strings from rail.js and who/state.js. A test reads those files, parses and runs them, and holds navManaged under a tenth of the walk's GROUP checks.

Left alone: When's per-keystroke search, Who's per-keystroke directory rebuild, trimming group columns, and share-card caching. descendants stays in the query language because the form without it was not a gain. Production timing of the descendant probe waits until deploy, because the live server answers 400 for the operator until then.

Merging main at the end found this branch already contained main (f686822). No conflicts. `go vet ./internal/db` and `go test ./internal/db` passed.

